1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,915
Hello, and welcome to our TensorForge 2.0 Phase 2 walkthrough.

2
00:00:03,915 --> 00:00:04,962
This is Fresh Desk:

3
00:00:04,962 --> 00:00:08,928
a customer support ticket classification and routing system for RideEat,

4
00:00:08,928 --> 00:00:12,618
the fictional ride-hailing and food delivery platform in the brief.

5
00:00:12,968 --> 00:00:17,443
In the next fifteen minutes we will show the working solution, explain the architecture,

6
00:00:17,443 --> 00:00:21,917
walk through how our three machine learning models were trained, validated and improved,

7
00:00:21,917 --> 00:00:26,696
and show exactly how we tested the authenticated evaluation API against the official contract.

8
00:00:28,246 --> 00:00:34,101
Each ticket arrives with a channel, which is email, chat or call transcript, an optional subject,

9
00:00:34,101 --> 00:00:34,946
and free text.

10
00:00:34,946 --> 00:00:40,258
The text can be English, Sinhala, Tamil, Sinhala or Tamil typed in Latin script, emojis,

11
00:00:40,258 --> 00:00:41,766
or a mix of all of these.

12
00:00:41,766 --> 00:00:45,448
First, we predict the primary category, one of eleven values.

13
00:00:45,798 --> 00:00:50,278
Second, a secondary category when the ticket clearly contains two issues, otherwise null.

14
00:00:50,278 --> 00:00:54,103
And third, whether the ticket is urgent and needs immediate human attention.

15
00:00:54,453 --> 00:00:55,775
The team is not predicted.

16
00:00:55,775 --> 00:01:01,012
It is derived from the category using the fixed table on the right, exactly as the contract defines it.

17
00:01:01,012 --> 00:01:03,096
The contract also adds consistency rules:

18
00:01:03,096 --> 00:01:05,740
the secondary category must differ from the primary,

19
00:01:05,740 --> 00:01:08,791
and spam is never urgent and never has a secondary category.

20
00:01:10,341 --> 00:01:14,626
The guidelines are clear that simply wrapping a large language model is discouraged.

21
00:01:14,626 --> 00:01:18,604
So every prediction in Fresh Desk comes from classifiers we trained ourselves:

22
00:01:18,604 --> 00:01:21,920
TF-IDF features over words and characters, feeding linear models,

23
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:25,898
trained only on the four thousand labelled tickets supplied by the organisers.

24
00:01:26,248 --> 00:01:29,539
We train three separate models, one for each prediction target.

25
00:01:29,539 --> 00:01:33,927
The fitted pipelines are saved as a single artifact and baked into the Docker image,

26
00:01:33,927 --> 00:01:37,950
so inference needs no internet, no model download and no external AI service.

27
00:01:37,950 --> 00:01:42,390
And every response is checked against the official OpenAPI contract and JSON Schemas.

28
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:45,298
Here is the architecture.

29
00:01:45,298 --> 00:01:46,493
There are two ways in.

30
00:01:46,493 --> 00:01:51,327
On the left, the judges' evaluation harness calls the API directly with the team API key.

31
00:01:51,327 --> 00:01:55,618
Below it, Fresh Desk has a regular workspace and a separate Judge demo console.

32
00:01:55,618 --> 00:01:58,606
The regular workspace uses a same-origin server bridge,

33
00:01:58,606 --> 00:02:01,593
so the configured server key never reaches the browser.

34
00:02:01,593 --> 00:02:06,754
In the Judge console, the evaluator enters their own key; it is verified by the API and held in

35
00:02:06,754 --> 00:02:08,492
page memory only until sign-out.

36
00:02:08,842 --> 00:02:13,611
Every protected request goes through the same checks, in the order the contract requires:

37
00:02:13,611 --> 00:02:19,398
authentication first, then content type, then payload size, then JSON parsing, and finally field validation.

38
00:02:19,398 --> 00:02:22,720
An unauthorised request never reaches the parser or the model.

39
00:02:23,070 --> 00:02:24,945
Valid tickets go to the ticket model:

40
00:02:24,945 --> 00:02:29,555
the three trained pipelines, plus the fixed category to team map and the consistency rules.

41
00:02:29,555 --> 00:02:32,595
The single and small batch endpoints run this synchronously.

42
00:02:32,945 --> 00:02:35,384
Large evaluation runs use asynchronous jobs.

43
00:02:35,384 --> 00:02:38,266
The request is validated up front, stored in SQLite,

44
00:02:38,266 --> 00:02:43,920
and a background worker classifies the tickets, so health checks and live predictions stay responsive.

45
00:02:44,270 --> 00:02:48,720
All of this ships as one Docker image that starts with a single docker run command and runs with no

46
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:49,708
network access at all.

47
00:02:51,258 --> 00:02:52,430
Let's see it working.

48
00:02:52,430 --> 00:02:57,508
This is the Fresh Desk home page, live on our hosted site at tensorforge-phase2.vercel.app,

49
00:02:57,508 --> 00:02:59,852
served by the same FastAPI app as the API.

50
00:02:59,852 --> 00:03:04,707
The status pill in the top right confirms the model is loaded and the service is ready.

51
00:03:05,057 --> 00:03:08,005
We open the desk and create a live chat ticket in Singlish:

52
00:03:08,005 --> 00:03:09,754
Sinhala typed with English letters.

53
00:03:09,754 --> 00:03:14,500
The customer says their order is an hour late, the driver won't answer, and they want a refund.

54
00:03:14,851 --> 00:03:16,804
The prediction comes straight back.

55
00:03:16,804 --> 00:03:17,752
Primary category:

56
00:03:17,752 --> 00:03:20,318
delivery delay, routed to Delivery Operations.

57
00:03:20,318 --> 00:03:21,378
Secondary category:

58
00:03:21,378 --> 00:03:25,227
payment refund, because the customer also asked for their money back.

59
00:03:25,227 --> 00:03:27,737
The urgency model marks it standard priority.

60
00:03:27,737 --> 00:03:30,303
Primary confidence is only thirty one percent,

61
00:03:30,303 --> 00:03:33,985
so Fresh Desk recommends human review before the ticket is routed.

62
00:03:34,335 --> 00:03:35,396
Next, a safety case.

63
00:03:35,396 --> 00:03:39,268
A passenger says the driver is not stopping the car, and they are scared.

64
00:03:39,268 --> 00:03:43,299
The model routes it to Trust and Safety, with ninety six percent confidence,

65
00:03:43,299 --> 00:03:47,648
and raises the urgent flag, so the ticket waits for a support agent to approve it.

66
00:03:55,059 --> 00:04:00,297
Now a message in Tamil script, about an item missing from an order and a request for the money back.

67
00:04:00,297 --> 00:04:04,015
It goes to Food Operations, with payment refund as the secondary issue.

68
00:04:04,015 --> 00:04:07,472
Confidence here is low, so the ticket is flagged for human review.

69
00:04:07,472 --> 00:04:08,990
There is no translation step:

70
00:04:08,990 --> 00:04:13,651
character n-gram features let one model read Sinhala, Tamil and English scripts directly.

71
00:04:16,327 --> 00:04:20,223
Every classified ticket lands in Live operations, a running view of routes,

72
00:04:20,223 --> 00:04:22,404
urgency and review status for the session.

73
00:04:23,983 --> 00:04:26,929
Urgent or low-confidence tickets go to the Support queue.

74
00:04:26,929 --> 00:04:29,979
Support staff sign in with a separate server-side password.

75
00:04:29,979 --> 00:04:32,614
The customer role cannot accept or reroute tickets.

76
00:04:32,964 --> 00:04:36,183
A support agent reviews the model's signals and accepts the ticket,

77
00:04:36,183 --> 00:04:39,352
or picks a different team and must give a reason for the override.

78
00:04:39,352 --> 00:04:41,706
The model's original prediction is never changed:

79
00:04:41,706 --> 00:04:43,627
the human decision is stored next to it.

80
00:04:57,388 --> 00:05:00,967
Next in the navigation is the Judge demo, built for evaluators.

81
00:05:00,967 --> 00:05:03,069
It gathers the evidence in one place:

82
00:05:03,069 --> 00:05:08,181
the live site, the hosted API, the contract tests, the repository, the offline Docker run,

83
00:05:08,181 --> 00:05:11,476
training and evaluation, the report, and this walkthrough.

84
00:05:11,827 --> 00:05:14,914
Below the checklist is every route in the evaluation contract,

85
00:05:14,914 --> 00:05:18,846
from the public health check to the authenticated prediction and job endpoints,

86
00:05:18,846 --> 00:05:21,236
with a link to the raw Open A P I specification.

87
00:05:21,585 --> 00:05:23,878
Judges sign in with their own evaluation key.

88
00:05:23,878 --> 00:05:29,429
The page checks it with the auth verify endpoint, and keeps it in page memory only, never in browser storage.

89
00:05:32,924 --> 00:05:34,892
The console then calls the real API.

90
00:05:34,892 --> 00:05:38,880
Here is a single prediction, with the full contract-shaped JSON response.

91
00:05:39,231 --> 00:05:43,184
And a synchronous batch, with one prediction per ticket ID, in order.

92
00:05:43,184 --> 00:05:49,372
From the same console, judges can also submit an asynchronous job, poll it, fetch its results, or cancel it.

93
00:05:50,922 --> 00:05:53,924
The Batch workbench is the evaluation path in the dashboard.

94
00:05:53,924 --> 00:05:57,927
Up to one hundred tickets run synchronously, through the predict batch endpoint.

95
00:05:57,927 --> 00:05:59,177
Here is the sample batch.

96
00:05:59,528 --> 00:06:03,987
The predictions come back in the same order as the request, each one echoing its ticket ID.

97
00:06:05,246 --> 00:06:06,800
Now a full synchronous batch:

98
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:11,245
we paste one hundred tickets, the largest batch the predict batch endpoint accepts.

99
00:06:11,245 --> 00:06:15,530
Anything larger, up to five thousand tickets, becomes an asynchronous batch job,

100
00:06:15,530 --> 00:06:18,208
which we show through the API later in this video.

101
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:36,183
All one hundred predictions are back, in order,

102
00:06:36,183 --> 00:06:40,219
and can be downloaded as JSON in the same format the judges receive from the API.

103
00:06:41,769 --> 00:06:43,640
Fresh Desk is also fully responsive.

104
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:45,719
Here is the same hosted site on a phone.

105
00:06:45,719 --> 00:06:50,605
The header, navigation and landing page reflow into a single column, with nothing hidden away.

106
00:06:51,449 --> 00:06:54,578
Support staff and customers can raise a ticket from their phone.

107
00:06:54,578 --> 00:06:58,929
This customer says the driver took a long route, charged them double, and wants a refund.

108
00:06:59,482 --> 00:07:01,730
The full prediction fits the small screen:

109
00:07:01,730 --> 00:07:05,477
Ride Operations as the route, ride trip issue as the primary category,

110
00:07:05,477 --> 00:07:08,741
payment refund as the secondary issue, and standard priority.

111
00:07:08,741 --> 00:07:12,648
Confidence is above fifty percent, so the ticket is routed automatically.

112
00:07:24,302 --> 00:07:25,915
Now, how the models were built.

113
00:07:25,915 --> 00:07:27,683
We used only the organisers' data:

114
00:07:27,683 --> 00:07:31,219
four thousand training tickets and eight hundred validation tickets.

115
00:07:31,219 --> 00:07:34,236
The archive was downloaded from the official Drive folder,

116
00:07:34,236 --> 00:07:37,824
and the SHA-256 checksum of every file is recorded in the repository.

117
00:07:38,174 --> 00:07:41,265
The models see only what the API will see at evaluation time:

118
00:07:41,265 --> 00:07:42,582
channel, subject and text.

119
00:07:42,582 --> 00:07:46,383
We deliberately excluded ticket IDs and the language labels in the dataset,

120
00:07:46,383 --> 00:07:51,804
because the judges' requests never contain them, and a model that relied on them would fail on the holdout.

121
00:07:52,154 --> 00:07:54,465
The validation set covers six language styles,

122
00:07:54,465 --> 00:07:59,538
from English and Singlish to Sinhala and Tamil script, with smaller Tanglish and mixed-script groups.

123
00:08:01,088 --> 00:08:02,788
Each target has its own pipeline.

124
00:08:02,788 --> 00:08:07,630
The category model is a class-balanced logistic regression over word unigrams and bigrams plus

125
00:08:07,630 --> 00:08:10,360
word-boundary character n-grams of length two to six.

126
00:08:10,360 --> 00:08:14,687
Character n-grams are what make the model work across scripts and spelling variants,

127
00:08:14,687 --> 00:08:16,902
such as the many ways people type Singlish.

128
00:08:17,252 --> 00:08:21,615
The secondary category model is a linear support vector machine with sigmoid calibration,

129
00:08:21,615 --> 00:08:23,135
so we get usable probabilities.

130
00:08:23,135 --> 00:08:27,890
A secondary category is only returned when its probability is above zero point two and it differs

131
00:08:27,890 --> 00:08:28,723
from the primary.

132
00:08:29,073 --> 00:08:33,019
The urgency model is another balanced logistic regression on character n-grams,

133
00:08:33,019 --> 00:08:37,415
with a decision threshold of zero point three, chosen to favour catching urgent tickets.

134
00:08:37,415 --> 00:08:39,863
For secondary and urgency we apply light cleanup:

135
00:08:39,863 --> 00:08:44,758
normalising whitespace and removing quoted earlier correspondence and synthetic reference numbers.

136
00:08:45,108 --> 00:08:47,726
After the models, fixed rules enforce the contract:

137
00:08:47,726 --> 00:08:50,858
the team comes from the category table, spam is never urgent,

138
00:08:50,858 --> 00:08:53,579
and the secondary category cannot repeat the primary.

139
00:08:53,579 --> 00:08:58,969
When confidence is below fifty percent or the message is three words or fewer, we recommend human review.

140
00:09:00,519 --> 00:09:04,567
Here is how the models were validated and improved, including what did not work.

141
00:09:04,567 --> 00:09:08,464
We started from a baseline at sixty two point nine percent category accuracy.

142
00:09:08,814 --> 00:09:12,239
Our first round of tuning used an internal split of the training data,

143
00:09:12,239 --> 00:09:15,419
where every candidate scored ninety-eight to ninety-nine percent.

144
00:09:15,419 --> 00:09:16,593
That was a warning sign:

145
00:09:16,593 --> 00:09:19,627
the synthetic training tickets are very similar to each other.

146
00:09:19,627 --> 00:09:24,373
Sure enough, the candidate we picked there dropped to sixty point nine percent on the development

147
00:09:24,373 --> 00:09:25,107
validation set.

148
00:09:25,107 --> 00:09:27,847
So we stopped trusting the internal split for selection.

149
00:09:28,197 --> 00:09:31,989
We also tried stripping greeting and sign-off boilerplate from the text.

150
00:09:31,989 --> 00:09:36,518
It sounded sensible, but accuracy fell to around fifty six percent, so we rejected it.

151
00:09:36,518 --> 00:09:38,940
Those polite phrases turn out to carry signal.

152
00:09:39,290 --> 00:09:41,570
We then compared regularisation strengths,

153
00:09:41,570 --> 00:09:44,828
class weighting and feature settings on the development set,

154
00:09:44,828 --> 00:09:49,823
kept the best primary configuration at sixty three percent accuracy and sixty five macro-F1,

155
00:09:49,823 --> 00:09:51,560
and swept the urgency threshold,

156
00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:56,012
which lifted urgency macro-F1 from ninety two point six to ninety three point one.

157
00:09:56,012 --> 00:10:00,030
Every experiment, including the rejected ones, is saved in the repository.

158
00:10:01,580 --> 00:10:05,685
These are the final scores on the eight hundred development validation tickets.

159
00:10:05,685 --> 00:10:09,167
Category accuracy is sixty three percent, with sixty five macro-F1.

160
00:10:09,167 --> 00:10:12,389
Secondary category accuracy is ninety five and a half percent,

161
00:10:12,389 --> 00:10:16,234
and urgency is ninety seven and a half percent with ninety three macro-F1.

162
00:10:16,234 --> 00:10:20,028
All eight hundred predictions validate against the official JSON Schemas.

163
00:10:20,378 --> 00:10:24,580
Per category, spam is perfect, and ride, account and delivery issues are strong.

164
00:10:24,580 --> 00:10:29,202
The weakest is order missing or wrong, which is most often confused with payment refund,

165
00:10:29,202 --> 00:10:32,301
since those customers usually ask for their money back too.

166
00:10:32,651 --> 00:10:35,871
By language, Singlish is the strongest at over ninety percent,

167
00:10:35,871 --> 00:10:39,194
while Sinhala script is the hardest at around forty six percent.

168
00:10:39,194 --> 00:10:40,233
We want to be clear:

169
00:10:40,233 --> 00:10:44,023
these are development scores, and the validation set informed our tuning.

170
00:10:44,023 --> 00:10:46,308
The judges' unseen holdout is the real test.

171
00:10:47,858 --> 00:10:50,996
Now the evaluation API, which the judges call directly.

172
00:10:50,996 --> 00:10:52,936
Health is public and needs no key.

173
00:10:53,286 --> 00:10:56,716
Every prediction and job endpoint requires the team API key.

174
00:10:56,716 --> 00:11:02,147
A missing key or a wrong key returns 401, with a JSON error body and a WWW-Authenticate header,

175
00:11:02,147 --> 00:11:04,262
before the request body is even read.

176
00:11:04,612 --> 00:11:09,086
With the right key, in either the X-API-Key header or an Authorization Bearer header,

177
00:11:09,086 --> 00:11:12,244
we get the prediction in exactly the contract's field names.

178
00:11:12,244 --> 00:11:17,085
The key is read from the API_KEY environment variable at startup, compared in constant time,

179
00:11:17,085 --> 00:11:19,401
never logged, and never sent to the browser.

180
00:11:19,401 --> 00:11:23,506
If no key is configured, the service refuses requests instead of running open.

181
00:11:25,056 --> 00:11:29,948
Robustness is part of the scoring, so invalid input always gets a clean JSON error.

182
00:11:29,948 --> 00:11:31,363
Malformed JSON gets 400.

183
00:11:31,363 --> 00:11:36,078
An unknown channel or empty text gets 422, with details for every failing field.

184
00:11:36,428 --> 00:11:38,231
A wrong content type gets 415.

185
00:11:38,231 --> 00:11:42,256
In a batch, a duplicate ticket ID fails the whole request with 422,

186
00:11:42,256 --> 00:11:45,140
and the details carry the index of the bad item.

187
00:11:45,490 --> 00:11:49,701
Even unknown routes return JSON, never an HTML page or a stack trace.

188
00:11:51,251 --> 00:11:56,532
For large evaluation runs, the judges submit up to five thousand tickets as an asynchronous job.

189
00:11:56,532 --> 00:12:02,032
We validate every ticket up front, then return 202 with a job ID, a Location header and Retry-After.

190
00:12:02,032 --> 00:12:06,983
Sending the same Idempotency-Key again returns the same job instead of creating a new one.

191
00:12:07,333 --> 00:12:10,499
Asking for results before the job has finished gives 409.

192
00:12:10,849 --> 00:12:14,974
Polling the status shows the job succeeded with three thousand of three thousand processed.

193
00:12:14,974 --> 00:12:18,328
Results come back in request order and can be paged with offset and limit.

194
00:12:18,678 --> 00:12:19,997
Unknown job IDs give 404.

195
00:12:19,997 --> 00:12:24,745
Jobs are stored in SQLite and kept for twenty four hours, well above the six hour minimum.

196
00:12:24,745 --> 00:12:29,070
We allow one running and three queued jobs, and if the container restarts mid-job,

197
00:12:29,070 --> 00:12:33,079
the job ends as failed with the reason interrupted, instead of disappearing.

198
00:12:34,629 --> 00:12:35,689
Next, the container.

199
00:12:35,689 --> 00:12:39,400
We build the image, then start it with networking completely disabled,

200
00:12:39,400 --> 00:12:42,050
and the API key passed as an environment variable.

201
00:12:42,050 --> 00:12:46,502
It is healthy in about two seconds, well inside the hundred and twenty second limit.

202
00:12:46,852 --> 00:12:49,958
Inside that offline container, health reports the loaded model,

203
00:12:49,958 --> 00:12:54,148
and a Sinhala ticket about a bag left in a car is correctly routed to Lost and Found.

204
00:12:54,498 --> 00:12:59,248
And to prove there really is no network, a request to the internet from inside the container fails.

205
00:12:59,248 --> 00:13:00,447
The model never needs it.

206
00:13:01,997 --> 00:13:07,136
This is the documented single start command, docker run with the port mapping and the API key.

207
00:13:07,136 --> 00:13:08,940
Health returns 200 straight away.

208
00:13:10,490 --> 00:13:12,485
Finally, how we tested all of this.

209
00:13:12,485 --> 00:13:16,761
Thirty nine automated tests cover the API, the job engine and the contract.

210
00:13:16,761 --> 00:13:22,518
They validate responses against both the OpenAPI file and the organisers' draft 2020-12 JSON Schemas.

211
00:13:22,868 --> 00:13:27,380
The evaluation script runs all eight hundred validation tickets and checks every prediction against

212
00:13:27,380 --> 00:13:28,337
the official schemas:

213
00:13:28,337 --> 00:13:30,159
eight hundred out of eight hundred pass.

214
00:13:30,509 --> 00:13:33,770
And our Postman collection behaves like the judges' harness:

215
00:13:33,770 --> 00:13:37,738
thirty five requests covering correct, wrong and missing keys, bad input,

216
00:13:37,738 --> 00:13:43,010
batches and a full async job, with over two thousand seven hundred assertions, and zero failures.

217
00:13:43,010 --> 00:13:45,619
The same collection runs against the hosted URL.

218
00:13:47,169 --> 00:13:48,896
To wrap up, our main findings.

219
00:13:48,896 --> 00:13:52,698
Character n-grams let one small, fast model handle Sinhala, Tamil,

220
00:13:52,698 --> 00:13:56,326
English and their Latin-script forms, with no translation step.

221
00:13:56,326 --> 00:14:02,546
And internal splits of synthetic data badly overstate accuracy, so model selection has to use held-out data.

222
00:14:02,896 --> 00:14:04,664
We are also honest about the limits.

223
00:14:04,664 --> 00:14:08,103
Primary category accuracy of sixty three percent is the main weakness,

224
00:14:08,103 --> 00:14:11,050
with missing-order versus refund being the hardest boundary,

225
00:14:11,050 --> 00:14:13,604
and Sinhala script being the hardest language group.

226
00:14:13,954 --> 00:14:16,502
That is why Fresh Desk keeps people in the loop.

227
00:14:16,502 --> 00:14:21,013
Review is recommended conservatively, on 485 of the eight hundred validation tickets,

228
00:14:21,013 --> 00:14:24,728
and a support agent always makes the final routing decision for those.

229
00:14:26,278 --> 00:14:28,662
Everything you have seen is in our repository:

230
00:14:28,662 --> 00:14:33,532
the training and evaluation scripts, the saved experiments, the tests, the Postman collection,

231
00:14:33,532 --> 00:14:35,087
